# Who is allowed to sign commits in this repository.
#
# IGN-SC-01 says nothing executes on a host unless its source is signed and
# verified against a pinned allowed-signers list. This is that list, and it is
# committed so that adding a signer is a reviewable diff rather than a change
# somebody makes to their own machine.
#
# Format is ssh-keygen(1) allowed_signers:
#
#     <principal> namespaces="git" <keytype> <key>
#
# The principal is the signing identity — normally the committer's email. The
# namespace restriction matters: without it a key trusted for git commits is
# also trusted for anything else ssh-keygen is asked to verify.
#
# To verify locally:
#
#     git config gpg.ssh.allowedSignersFile .allowed_signers
#     git log --show-signature
#
# To add yourself, in the same pull request as your first signed commit:
#
#     1. Add your public key below.
#     2. Register the same key on your Git host as a *signing* key. A key
#        registered only for authentication will sign fine and verify nowhere.
#
# Removing a key here does not invalidate history that was signed with it. It
# stops that key signing anything new, which is the property offboarding needs.

